{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "Which incident topics/themes warrant analyst attention (attention-worthiness derived from incident short_description)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "topic",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Infers the semantic theme of each incident from its description, establishing the categorical basis of 'what' the incident is about -- the core substrate for identifying what's worth attention."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_high_frequency_topic",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Derives whether a topic is common vs. rare, a facet of attention (volume-driven prioritization) that builds directly on the topic dimension."
    },
    {
      "name": "attention_flag",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Directly operationalizes the attention-worthiness concept into a graded signal (Routine/Medium/High/Low-Anomaly), which is the explicit target the query asks about."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.85,
  "structure_quality": 0.78,
  "focus_actionability": 0.82,
  "rationale": "The three columns centre coherently on a single inferable focus: attention-worthiness of incidents grounded in their descriptive topic. 'topic' extracts the theme, 'is_high_frequency_topic' adds a volume facet, and 'attention_flag' synthesizes the priority signal -- a sound theme -> frequency -> attention structure rather than a grab-bag. Structure is slightly weakened because 'is_high_frequency_topic' is a coarse binary largely redundant with topic frequency, and 'attention_flag' bundles anomaly and severity into one label without exposing its drivers, limiting analytical granularity. Still, the implied focus is genuinely actionable: an analyst can triage High/Low-Anomaly incidents and investigate high-frequency topics, so it is neither vacuous nor a mere restatement. It is derived from real content (descriptions) rather than mechanical text metrics."
}
```

